  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mild and dry this week. Record breaking highs \nare possible mid-week. \n\nThe sfc low from the remnants of Isaias slides offshore early \nthis morning. Behind it, strong high pressure over the gulf \nridges north to cover the eastern half of the CONUS. Scattered \nshowers are possible across northern portions of the area on the\nbackside of the low early this morning. Precip could linger \nover the MD Eastern Shore as late as mid-morning, but otherwise \ndrying out. \n\nThe pattern shifts to a warm and dry one for the week as high \npressure to the SW remains in control. Aloft, a strong ridge builds \nover the central CONUS and slowly expands eastward through midweek. \nThis set up will result in a warming trend that will peak on \nWednesday with potentially record-challenging highs. Highs today \nwill be in the upper 70s inland and the low-mid 70s at the coast. \nTues highs will be in the upper 70s to low 80s. Wednesday's highs \nwill be in the upper 80s to around 90. There is some disagreement in \nthe global guidance regarding the strength of the ridge by the time \nThurs/Fri comes around, but they all more or less show it breaking \ndown late week as a trough starts to take shape over the upper \nmidwest. A weak backdoor front may try to push south Thurs, but \nthere is of course uncertainty as to how far it will get before \nstalling out, which means temperatures are uncertain as well. \nCurrently looks like a mostly dry front, but a few showers are \npossible across the N Thurs. The backdoor front would be pushed back \nto the N on Fri, resulting in one more warm day with highs in the \n80s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much cooler temperatures over the weekend. The \nfirst frost advisories of the season are possible Saturday night. \n\nThere is fairly good agreement between the global deterministic and \nensemble models regarding the stark pattern shift going into the \nweekend. All depict a deepening UL trough pivoting into the Mid-\nAtlantic late Friday into Saturday with cool sfc high pressure \nbuilding in behind a strong cold front. So far it doesn't look like \na very wet front with only isolated to widely scattered showers \nforecast. Highs on Saturday will be much cooler with most places \nstruggling to leave the 50s, and the same goes for Sunday. It looks \nlike Saturday night will be the one to watch for the first frost of \nthe season and Sunday night could be the second frost right after. \nLows in the upper 30s for the piedmont and mid 40s in the east are \ncurrently forecast each night, but would not be surprised to see the \nlow to mid 30s, especially Sunday night when the high is directly \noverhead.",
